(a) A utility who has discontinued or is discontinuing operation of any facilities in the city shall:
(1) Provide information satisfactory to the city that the utility's obligations for its facilities in the rights-of-way under this article and any other provision in the codified ordinances or other laws have been lawfully assumed by another utility;
(2) Submit a written proposal to re-use its facilities;
(3) Submit a written proposal for abandonment of facilities which must be approved by the public works director;
(4) Remove its entire facilities within a reasonable amount of time and in a manner acceptable to the city; or
(5) Submit to the city, in good faith and within a reasonable amount of time, a proposal for transferring ownership of its facilities of the city. If a utility proceeds to transfer ownership to the city, the city may, at its option do one or more of the following:
a. Purchase the facilities;
b. Accept donation of some or all facilities; or
c. Require the utility to post a bond in an amount sufficient to reimburse the city for its reasonably anticipated costs to be incurred in removing the facilities.
(b) Facilities of a utility who fails to comply with the above provision shall be deemed to be abandoned. Abandoned facilities are deemed to be a nuisance. The city may exercise any remedies or rights it has at law or in equity, including, but not limited to abating the nuisance; taking possession of the facilities; evicting the utility from the right-of-way prosecuting the violator; and/or any other remedy provided by city ordinance or otherwise at law or in equity.
(Ord. No. 2017-10, § 1, 5-16-2017)

549:
(a) The city manager or city manager's designee shall prepare a questionnaire for the purpose of obtaining pertinent information regarding the physical description, identity, and background of each applicant for a peddling permit. A peddling permit application shall require a minimum of a 14-day processing period to allow the city to receive and review the required reports and to have photographic identifications badges produced by a third-party vendor. The questionnaire may include the following:
(1) Name, local address and telephone number;
(2) Date and place of birth;
(3) Driver's license number and issuing state or current valid government issued identification with photo;
(4) Description of the kind and nature of the property, product, goods, wares, merchandise or services sold or solicited for sale by the applicant;
(5) Height, weight, eye color and hair color of the applicant;
(6) Reserved;
(7) Name, address and telephone number of the organization or business represented;
(8) Name and telephone number of immediate supervisors;
(9) Health department permit and state sales tax number, if applicable; and
(10) A list of all criminal arrests, convictions and the disposition of each and every charge in any federal, state, or municipal court, other than minor traffic violations.
(11) Disclosure of whether the applicant has been refused a state or municipal permit of any kind by another jurisdiction along with stating the reasons for such refusal.
A response to any item on the application which indicates that the applicant has violated a prior permit, solicited in any city without a required permit, previously violated the requirements of this section, pled guilty or been convicted of a crime may result in disqualification.
(b) The questionnaire form shall also bear the following statement: "O.C.G.A. § 16-10-71, provides that a person who makes a lawful oath or affirmation or who executes a document knowing that it purports to be an acknowledgement of a lawful oath or affirmation commits the offense of false swearing when, in any matter or thing other than a judicial proceeding, he or she knowingly and willfully makes a false statement."
(c) An application and permit fee as stated on the current fee schedule kept on file with the city clerk shall be charged annually for this permit at the time the application is filed. Upon payment of the required permit fee and completing the questionnaire, the city manager or the city manager's designee shall review the application and may perform a criminal background check on the applicant for the purpose of ascertaining whether the applicant has pled to or has been convicted of a felony, or a misdemeanor involving violence or moral turpitude. A plea or conviction of a felony including a sentence imposed under the first offender statute, or a misdemeanor involving violence or moral turpitude shall disqualify the applicant. An applicant is ineligible for a peddling permit if any of the following items have been done:
(1) Applicant failed to complete the entire application correctly.
(2) Applicant submitted false or inaccurate information of any kind.
(3) Applicant failed to provide for payment of the application fee for any reason.
(4) Applicant has violated the terms of this article in prior years.
If the city manager or the city manager's designee determines that the applicant is ineligible, the applicant shall be notified in writing of the denial of the permit. The city manager or the city manager's designee shall include in this notice the grounds for denial. Once a peddling permit is issued, no peddler shall engage in any activity without wearing and displaying his/her peddler permit badge in a conspicuous manner. The applicant may appeal a denial of a permit to the city council if the appeal is filed in writing with the city manager or the city manager's designee within 15 days of the date notice is sent of the denial.
(d) Penalty for violation of article. It shall be a violation of this article and grounds for revocation of an existing permit if the city manager or the city manager's designee finds any of the following:
(1) Any person, business, partnership, corporation, association, organization, or a group of persons required by this section to have a peddling permit engages in any peddling without first obtaining a permit or otherwise failing to comply with the requirements of this section;
(2) For any person to violate any of the provisions of this section or to violate any other city ordinance while engaging in any peddling;
(3) For any person to lend, rent or sell his peddling permit card to another;
(4) For any person engaged in peddling to misrepresent the purpose of his/her product or use any false or deceptive statement or misrepresentation to induce a sale or contribution, or use any plan, scheme, or ruse which misrepresents the status or purpose of the person making the call.
(e) Appeals to city council. An applicant for a peddling permit or a permit holder may appeal a decision of the city manager or the city manager's designee to deny or revoke a permit issued pursuant to this article. When acting as a board to review appeals under this section, the city council shall be a quasi-judicial body to receive all relevant evidence, hear witnesses, allow for cross examination and decide issues of law in consultation with the city attorney. The city council shall issue a written ruling which affirms, modifies, or reverses the action of the city manager or the city manager's designee. All notices shall be given to the appealing party at the address shown on the application.
(f) Exemptions.
(1) Persons, businesses and organizations engaging in religious, nonprofit, charitable or political activities involving peddling shall be exempt from the requirements of this article.
(2) Persons engaging peddling on behalf of a legally established nonprofit organization, school group, and civic organization shall be exempt from the requirements in this article.
(3) Peddlers that are participating in a city special event, city sanctioned festival or other event that is sanctioned by the city are exempt from the requirements of this article.
(4) Persons who offer plants, fruits, vegetables or livestock for sale which are grown or raised on the same property where the selling is being done are exempt from the provisions of this article.
(5) Yard sales are exempt from the provisions of this article. Yard sales are regulated by other provisions of this Code to limit the number to five yard sale days per zoning lot per year. In addition, any city sanctioned city-wide yard sale is exempt from this article.
(6) A flea market is exempt from the provisions of this article. A "flea market" is a sale of goods organized by an existing business that is licensed and permitted by the city provided the flea market is conducted on the property of the licensed and permitted business of the applicant and the event only occurs for one day per month.
(7) An existing city permitted business with a permanent business location may allow peddlers to set up on the property where the permanent business is located so long as the use conforms with the city zoning code related to the peddler's business. Such peddlers are exempt from complying with this article as long as they do not violate any parking, access, setback, or other zoning site requirements. Any city permitted business allowing peddlers to set up under the exemption shall be limited to allow only two peddlers per day.
(Ord. No. 2019-4, § 3, 6-18-2019; Ord. No. 2022-2, § 3, 4-19-2022)

